      "title": "Anti-Mirror",
      "topics": [],
      "fastest": true,
      "reverse": false,
      "shortest": false,
      "solution": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nchar mirror(char c) {\n    switch (c) {\n        case 'b': return 'd';\n        case 'd': return 'b';\n        case 'p': return 'q';\n        case 'q': return 'p';\n        case '(': return ')';\n        case ')': return '(';\n        case '[': return ']';\n        case ']': return '[';\n        case '{': return '}';\n        case '}': return '{';\n        default: return c;\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    char S[31];\n\n    scanf(\" %[^\\n]\", S);\n    int N = strlen(S);\n    for (int i = N - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        putchar(mirror(S[i]));\n    }\n\n    putchar('\\n');\n    return 0;\n}",
      "statement": "Given a string [[s]], reverse it.\n\nThen replace each mirrored character according to the following table:\n\n`b ↔ d`  `p ↔ q`   `( ↔ )`   `[ ↔ ]`   `{ ↔ }`\nAll other characters remain unchanged.\n\nPrint the resulting string.",
